<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Renewable Energy – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.2em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Renewable Energy in Panama</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Why Is Renewable Energy Important for Panama?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Clarify written instructions for peers. Communication: Participate in conflict resolution strategies with peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can help clarify misunderstandings about energy sources by combining gestures and visual aids to make complex instructions easier to understand (e.g., using hand motions to show how solar panels collect sunlight alongside pictures or diagrams). Can mediate disagreements in group projects by suggesting taking turns and encouraging raising their hands to ask questions (e.g., suggests each peer takes turns sharing their energy-saving ideas, or says, "Why don't we all raise our hands to ask questions before voting on the best idea?"). Can help resolve misunderstandings about energy sources by using gestures alone to explain key concepts (e.g., using hand movements to demonstrate how a dam holds water to generate electricity).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various simple and slightly complex diagrams/pictures related to renewable energy sources (e.g., a solar panel, a wind turbine, a hydropower dam). Students, in pairs, identify each source and briefly discuss what they already know. The teacher prompts with questions like, "What is **generating** energy here?" or "What do you think is **happening** in this picture?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to clarify a technical concept (e.g., "How does a solar panel convert sunlight into electricity?") using gestures and simplified language. The teacher says, "A solar panel works by **harnessing** sunlight (gestures capturing light) and **converting** it into usable **electricity** for homes or schools (gestures connecting to a house). So, **generating** clean **energy** is the main goal because it **preserves** our **environment**." The teacher models emphasizing stress in "renewable" and "preservation."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher asks, "When I explained how a solar panel works, what did I do to make it easier to understand for you?" (Expected answer: "You used your hands/gestures.") "Why is it important to explain things clearly to our peers, especially for a project?" (Expected answer: "So everyone understands and we don't have disagreements.")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Renewable Energy in Panama".</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short, written excerpt from the 'School Renewable Energy Proposal Guide'. This guide includes instructions for proposing energy solutions, a brief overview of the school's current energy **consumption**, and a list of approved renewable **technology** for consideration (e.g., solar **panels**, small wind **turbines**). The text contains phrases using gerunds ("**Installing** efficient lighting...") and adverbial clauses ("...because it **reduces reliance** on fossil fuels.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Can clarify misunderstandings about energy sources by combining gestures and visual aids to make complex instructions easier to understand.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the text individually.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is the main purpose of this guide?" "What kind of **energy** does the school primarily use right now?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed questions for mediation focus:</b> "Which part of the instructions do you think a classmate might find confusing?" "Find a sentence that uses an adverbial clause to justify an action. How would you rephrase it simply?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Mediation.</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using gerunds, present continuous, and adverbial clauses to explain and justify, while practicing clarification and conflict resolution strategies. Vocabulary: **source, efficiency, sustainability, preserve, generate, consume, invest, agree.** Pronunciation: Stress in "renewable," "preservation."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Rephrasing for Clarity:</b> In pairs, students receive a worksheet with complex sentences from the 'School Renewable Energy Proposal Guide' text. They must rephrase these sentences using simpler vocabulary and the target grammar (gerunds, present continuous, adverbial clauses). For example, "The school **is investigating** new infrastructure for energy **preservation** because it is a **cost-effective** strategy." becomes "The school **is looking into** new ways to **save** energy **because** it's a good way to save money." (Focus on /e/ in "pen" and /eɪ/ in "pain" when practicing "investigating" vs. "saving").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Explaining with Gestures:</b> Students return to their pairs. One student picks a concept or instruction from the proposal guide (e.g., "how solar panels **collect** sunlight") and explains it to their partner, specifically focusing on using gestures and simple language. The other student listens and identifies if they understood clearly. Teacher provides sentence starters for mediation: "Another way to say this is...", "I can show you with my hands...", "This means... **because**..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – Mediating Disagreements:</b> The teacher provides scenarios of minor disagreements related to the 'Renewable Energy Proposal' project (e.g., "One group member wants only solar panels, another wants wind turbines, and they can't **agree**."). Students practice mediating the disagreement in pairs, using phrases like, "Why don't we all raise our hands to ask questions before deciding?" or "Perhaps we can consider both options since **investing** in diverse sources is more **sustainable**."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Renewable Energy in Panama".</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Group Presentation & Clarification Session (21st Century Skills Project: Renewable Energy Proposal). In their established groups, students present a section of their "Renewable Energy Proposal" to the class. Each group identifies a 'mediator' for the presentation. During their presentation, other groups are encouraged to ask questions, some intentionally phrased to be slightly confusing or to challenge a point, prompting the 'mediator' to clarify.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups present their chosen renewable energy source for the school, explaining "Why **is renewable energy important** for Panama?" and specifically for their school. They use visual aids (diagrams, pictures of solar panels/wind turbines) to support their points.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The 'mediator' within each group is responsible for clarifying any technical terms, complex instructions, or misunderstandings that arise from audience questions. They must use rephrasing, gestures (e.g., hand movements to demonstrate how a dam holds water), and connect ideas using adverbial clauses (e.g., "We are suggesting solar panels **because** they are **cost-effective** and **preserve** the **environment**."). They also use gerunds (e.g., "**Installing** solar panels **is** a key step").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">If a minor disagreement arises within the presenting group (e.g., about the best **source**), the mediator demonstrates conflict resolution strategies (e.g., "Let's take turns explaining our points," or "Can we find a solution that helps us all **agree**?").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment (using a simple rubric).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students continue their "Renewable Energy Proposal" presentations, focusing on their individual and group mediation skills. Peers use a checklist to evaluate the mediator's effectiveness. The teacher observes and takes notes on each student's ability to clarify and mediate.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and effectiveness of explanations when clarifying information (e.g., rephrasing, simplifying terms).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of gestures and visual aids to enhance understanding of **energy** concepts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of target vocabulary (**renewable, sustainable, generate, efficiency, preserve**) and grammar (gerunds: "**Saving** **energy** is crucial"; present continuous: "We **are implementing** a plan"; adverbial clauses: "**because** it **reduces reliance** on **fossil fuels**").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrated ability to participate in conflict resolution strategies (e.g., suggesting turns, encouraging questions, finding common ground).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation with focus on stress in multisyllabic words (e.g., "**sus**tainable," "preser**va**tion").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief reflection:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How effectively did I clarify information to my peers today using gestures or different words?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one strategy I used to help my group resolve a disagreement during our project planning?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of clarifying complex information or mediating a discussion about our renewable energy project? How did I overcome it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can improving my mediation skills help me in other school projects or outside of class, for example, when discussing important topics like climate **preservation**?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did students successfully clarify information and mediate discussions using target language and strategies? Were the instructions for the project clear enough to prompt authentic mediation? What specific grammar points (gerunds, present continuous, adverbial clauses) or vocabulary (**sustainability, efficiency**) need more reinforcement?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I better scaffold mediation for conflict resolution next time, perhaps by providing more explicit phrases or role-play scenarios?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes our theme on "Why Is Renewable Energy Important for Panama?" and the "Renewable Energy Proposal" project. The mediation skills practiced will be crucial as we transition to our next theme, which will involve more collaborative problem-solving and discussions on global challenges.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>